WEBVTT

1
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.040
Estonia and the sea.

2
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.320
It could be that they are connected

3
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.400
from the beginning of time.
And they are.

4
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.080
It is also true that maritime affairs
have always been very international.

5
00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.880
A historian and an archaeologist
deal with the past of society.

6
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.840
One has material sources,
the other has written sources.

7
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.840
Time capsules as such

8
00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.360
have emerged quite a lot during
this time and are quite different.

9
00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:31.560
TIME CAPSULES:
THE STORY OF THE MARITIME MUSEUM

10
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.320
What’s here?
We couldn’t get pass the press,

11
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.480
the oldest newspaper <i>Postimees</i> .
Let’s put that in as well.

12
00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:50.920
I will put the <i>Meremees</i> .

13
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.920
Since the newspaper <i>Meremees</i>
is also very closely related…

14
00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.600
…to maritime affairs.

15
00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.480
First time in my life…

16
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.680
There you go.

17
00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.800
And we will also put
our cog in there.

18
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:15.920
At first sight it may not be much,

19
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.040
but we had to make it as small
as possible with a 3D printer

20
00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:23.280
to fit in our capsule.
- Let’s swim the boat.

21
00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.160
The builder puts the memory stick…
- We need to remember.

22
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:33.280
Instruction for the future generations
on how to unlock this memory stick.

23
00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.320
Very good.

24
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.560
Now let’s drop it down.

25
00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:54.000
FIRST CAPSULE:
THE MUSEUM

26
00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:11.360
Maritime museums as such
began to emerge

27
00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:16.160
in the surrounding countries
somewhere in the late 19th century.

28
00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.840
Now if we talk about Estonia,

29
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:24.280
it was the beginning of the 1930s.

30
00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:27.120
A period when the idea
of a maritime museum

31
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.400
began to emerge in Estonia
for the first time

32
00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.080
and here was a kind of
initiative group of the time

33
00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:37.760
that was behind the idea
of this Maritime Museum initiative.

34
00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:41.880
<i>The Maritime Museum has memorabilia</i>
related to maritime affairs.

35
00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:46.000
The Maritime Museum
of that time was opened in Tallinn.

36
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.440
Who now knows where
Terminal D is,

37
00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:52.480
then just before heading
towards the city centre

38
00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:54.920
there was a warehouse,
almost 300 square metres.

39
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.200
It was on the second floor.

40
00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.200
There was only one employee,
captain Madis Mei.

41
00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:04.600
<i>In the museum’s display</i>
we can see a model of a clipper,

42
00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:09.120
<i>the so-called brig,</i>
the Peipsi barge

43
00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.760
<i>and a model of the Chinese junk.</i>

44
00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.680
The quantity of items ranged
from a two to three thousand.

45
00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:21.880
The number of the first visitors
was probably about the same.

46
00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.440
But something
was already accomplished.

47
00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:27.240
<i>The icebreakers</i>
were replaced by a large saw

48
00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:30.000
<i>which was used to make</i>
way into the ice for ships.

49
00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:32.280
<i>And now the modern</i>
icebreaker Suur Tõll,

50
00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.440
<i>the model of which we are seeing,</i>
is breaking the way.

51
00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:43.000
The Maritime Museum operated
in a place called Paikov Bridge,

52
00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:46.240
in this warehouse until 1940

53
00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:50.080
with its only employee,
captain Madis Mei.

54
00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:57.200
Thankfully much of the collection
was packed before the war started.

55
00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:00.640
It didn’t matter what kind
of military action was coming,

56
00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:04.280
who is in power somewhere,
the port will still be hit.

57
00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:08.200
Fortunately, the collections
were packed up.

58
00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:11.760
They were scattered
in different parts of Tallinn,

59
00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:14.480
in basements and other museums.

60
00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:20.040
Due to this, a large part of
these exhibits have been preserved.

61
00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.440
<i>Congratulations</i>
on the 48th anniversary

62
00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:36.400
<i>of the great October Revolution!</i>

63
00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:38.480
<i>Hooray!</i>

64
00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.800
During the Soviet era in the 1940s,

65
00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:48.960
no one didn’t think to restore
or open the Maritime Museum.

66
00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.880
These thoughts came later.

67
00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.560
<i>The exhibits tell the story of</i>
a heroic past of the Baltic fleet.

68
00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.400
<i>Today we meet here a man who has with</i>
a gun in hand written the history.

69
00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.600
A maritime department was established
at the Tallinn City Museum.

70
00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.920
It was such a happy coincidence

71
00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.240
that there was Ants Pärna,
who came from the sea

72
00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.760
and had graduated
from a maritime school.

73
00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:20.440
He took a break from being on the sea

74
00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.120
and wanted to try
to establish the Maritime Museum

75
00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:27.320
and deal with maritime affairs
on the land.

76
00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:31.440
In 1965, at 70 Pikk street

77
00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:35.560
the new exposition of the
Maritime Museum was opened.

78
00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:41.240
Already in the 1970s
Ants Pärna started to think

79
00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:46.440
that next to it stands a large empty
artillery tower Fat Margaret.

80
00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:49.720
It could be used as well.

81
00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.520
In the mid-1970s,
when Tallinn was chosen

82
00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:58.960
as the city for the Moscow
Olympic Games sailing regatta

83
00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:01.280
things started to move.

84
00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:04.400
<i>Fat Margaret.</i>

85
00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:07.800
<i>Hanseatic cogs,</i>
sailing and steamships

86
00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:10.880
<i>have arrived here behind</i>
the gates for centuries.

87
00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.560
<i>The Tallinn Maritime Museum</i>
opened its doors.

88
00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:22.080
<i>Although gates would be</i>
more accurate than doors.

89
00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:26.440
Fat Margaret became a major
extension of the Maritime Museum

90
00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:31.920
and its grand opening
took place in April 1981.

91
00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:36.440
<i>Exhibits are from</i>
ancient times to the present day.

92
00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:40.720
<i>Like Fat Margaret itself:</i>
ancient limestone on the outside,

93
00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:44.040
<i>but modern ferroconcrete</i>
from the inside.

94
00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:22.640
We have held a lot of meetings

95
00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:25.520
for Fat Margareta’s new exhibition.

96
00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:28.080
For many years.

97
00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:32.840
Fortunately,
it could wait for its time.

98
00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:35.360
<i>Three shipwrecks</i>
were found in the earth

99
00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.920
<i>in Kadriorg during</i>
the construction works.

100
00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:39.800
<i>Due to construction</i>

101
00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:44.520
<i>the wrecks will be initially moved</i>
to the seabed for preservation.

102
00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:49.800
<i>A more precise dating will be</i>
clear probably in about 2–3 months.

103
00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:54.880
<i>But initial estimation could be</i>
the Middle Ages or the Early Modern,

104
00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.520
<i>until the 17th century.</i>

105
00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:00.520
In the summer,
when the ship was found,

106
00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:03.440
the Maritime Museum
did not have a clear plan

107
00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.560
that the ship
should go to Fat Margaret.

108
00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:07.680
Our plan was to
get it out of there.

109
00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.720
We did not know the consistency
of the ship’s wood,

110
00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:15.080
how long the ship
should be preserved.

111
00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:20.520
<i>For example, the conservation of</i>
the Bremen cog lasted 18 years.

112
00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.840
<i>I think we’re going to be faster.</i>

113
00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:26.520
And it wasn’t until the autumn

114
00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:30.600
that some idea began
to emerge to connect the cog

115
00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:33.480
with the Old Town,
with Fat Margaret and its renovation.

116
00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:38.800
The renovation plan needed to have
a cherry on top

117
00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:42.160
that would be a real hit.

118
00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:45.400
The place where I stand now

119
00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:49.920
is actually much higher
than it should be in the future.

120
00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.640
We should go about
2 metres down from here.

121
00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:59.480
And if I were to stand here,
2 metres deeper,

122
00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:04.480
and look up to the right,
I would see a cog above me.

123
00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:13.960
This room will not remain as tall
and spacious as it seems now.

124
00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:17.600
Another partition ceiling
will be built over my head,

125
00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:24.080
and from the ground floor here
it is possible to take the elevator

126
00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:26.600
if necessary,
even up to the roof

127
00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:30.000
to the roof café
or to the following floors.

128
00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:33.160
Second floor and sailing.

129
00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:39.440
First, there should be a big beautiful
showcase full of sailing ship models.

130
00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:52.880
We have reached the fourth floor.
The largest, widest floor.

131
00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:56.720
A partition ceiling will be
built here too, or rather the roof.

132
00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:02.400
Here comes the roof terrace with
a summer café and a viewing platform.

133
00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:05.960
There will also be a separate
exhibition on the roof.

134
00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:14.120
When someone thinks how will Fat
Margaret change from the outside,

135
00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:16.840
the best answer is
that nothing really changes.

136
00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:18.840
The whole change is inside.

137
00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:28.240
SECOND CAPSULE:
HANSEATIC COG

138
00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:49.080
It’s basically more or less…

139
00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:52.520
No, it’s under this house there.

140
00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:54.240
It was a rescue excavation,

141
00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:58.160
very time-critical,
very important findings.

142
00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:01.040
<i>How the work is done.</i>

143
00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:06.400
Or as it seems, not done.

144
00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:12.880
<i>Small piece of leather.</i>

145
00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:16.880
<i>Don’t go deeper,</i>
I’ll take samples later.

146
00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:20.880
We received
the information rather quickly

147
00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:24.200
that a couple of ships
had been found in Kadriorg.

148
00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:28.080
First, the so-called
Viljo wreck was excavated,

149
00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:31.400
which is a bit newer
and has survived less.

150
00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:36.160
After a few days when we went back,
the cog started to reveal itself as well.

151
00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:40.480
Where we are now
is the former seabed.

152
00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:43.560
Here was the sea.
Of course not anymore.

153
00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:48.960
The coastline extended
out to the Narva road.

154
00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:53.480
The soil that was brought in,

155
00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:57.480
consisting of urban waste
and factory ash and the like,

156
00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:00.160
probably retained the ship very well.

157
00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:05.400
When it was clear

158
00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:10.400
that it could belong to the cog
in terms of the type of ship,

159
00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:12.560
then seeing every day

160
00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:16.240
that more and more
interesting finds are coming out…

161
00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.400
<i>Got it.</i>
- We have to see

162
00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.800
<i>if it’s paired with</i>
the same shoe we got from here.

163
00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:25.400
…then we already felt that in
any case we would not want to

164
00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:29.040
take this wreck to the Baltic Sea
or to the Gulf of Finland,

165
00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:32.480
where we have a conservation area.
<i>- It’s moving.</i>

166
00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:35.000
In the evening I called
Urmas and asked.

167
00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:39.120
Because I really have
every museum’s dream here.

168
00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:43.480
A wreck of a cog,
with a great deal of findings.

169
00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:47.040
It was clear that this
was something very rare.

170
00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:49.160
However, it was not at all clear

171
00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:52.440
whether this would play any role
for the Maritime Museum.

172
00:14:56.680 --> 00:15:00.680
A great many of such findings
are taken to pieces when digging.

173
00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:04.120
Because it stayed together very
nicely and was so much preserved,

174
00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:07.200
then when it was decided
for the Maritime Museum,

175
00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:10.160
a support frame was built on it
during the excavations,

176
00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:12.200
so that it would stay together.

177
00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:17.320
And then it was brought
to the museum at night.

178
00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:26.680
The first plan was to treat it
like any wood found in the water.

179
00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:29.520
Immerse it in a preservative solution

180
00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:31.800
and then it would
stay there for years.

181
00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:35.360
Then this solution must be
altered and stirred all the time.

182
00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:39.720
Huge workload, very complex
and requires special equipment.

183
00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:43.440
We did not act only
by our own wisdom.

184
00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:47.120
Conservators from Tallinn
and Tartu visited.

185
00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:51.000
It was the English who were
immediately ready to come and see it.

186
00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:53.480
And people came
and took wood samples,

187
00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:56.440
which were analysed
in two laboratories.

188
00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:17.400
The question is how much
of the tar we should remove.

189
00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:21.280
Because from there
I removed quite a lot.

190
00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:24.080
Actually, as much as I could.

191
00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:26.480
Well, let’s have a look.

192
00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:28.080
These are difficult.

193
00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:32.160
These places are very difficult.

194
00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:36.440
I have not touched those as much.

195
00:16:36.520 --> 00:16:40.080
And when the first results of the
analysis began to come back,

196
00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:42.520
that this wood is actually
very well preserved,

197
00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:44.520
then it built a bit more courage.

198
00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:47.560
It looks like this ship
is going to endure.

199
00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:51.600
<i>A number of artifacts were found</i>
from the 14th century wreck,

200
00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:55.360
<i>which will give archaeologists</i>
a lot of research opportunities.

201
00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:59.640
<i>According to Maili Roio</i>
from the National Heritage Board,

202
00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:02.680
<i>this is a cog type</i>
of Hanseatic cargo ship,

203
00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:06.200
<i>which probably comes from the</i>
southern shores of the Baltic Sea.

204
00:17:08.440 --> 00:17:12.840
In fact, information about
the objects first came to light

205
00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:19.480
that these earthenware dates from
the first half of the 14th century.

206
00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:22.560
The textile is as it is.

207
00:17:23.320 --> 00:17:26.080
All the ropes and other things
that were found.

208
00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:30.560
Then dendroanalyses came from Tartu.

209
00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:35.560
As the tree grows,
one annual ring is formed each year.

210
00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:38.520
If it is a good year,
then this ring is thicker,

211
00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:42.840
if it is a drier year for the tree,
then this ring is thinner.

212
00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:49.960
It is clear that the annual rings
of the old tree

213
00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:52.520
and the younger tree overlap.

214
00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:57.560
In this way, by counting the rings
together and going back in time,

215
00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:01.800
it is possible to say the year when
this particular tree stopped growing

216
00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:04.360
and to find out the origin.

217
00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:07.840
Feels like it’s going through.

218
00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:10.440
All the way through?

219
00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:13.640
But I couldn’t see
anything coming through.

220
00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:16.800
It should be the joint
of two planks.

221
00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:22.120
Yes, but it is like…
- The end seems to be here.

222
00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:28.440
Yes, you are right.
- Planks are overlapping.

223
00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:31.160
Exactly.

224
00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:35.360
It then turned out that it was
the modern Polish region

225
00:18:35.440 --> 00:18:38.680
and year is 1296,

226
00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:42.360
which is the so-called
terminus post quem.

227
00:18:42.840 --> 00:18:46.880
In other words,
this is the time after which

228
00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:50.240
this tree or these trees
had to be cut down.

229
00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:11.520
Iron nodule, when it’s corroding.
Here as well. - I can see.

230
00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:23.200
All these will fall off.

231
00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:29.280
It is covered
with corrosion and sand.

232
00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:53.600
This looks very good.

233
00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:56.440
One thing that I think is important

234
00:19:56.520 --> 00:20:01.040
for the cog and for the history
of Estonian shipping in general

235
00:20:01.400 --> 00:20:07.400
is that there is such a huge gap
in the history of Estonian shipping

236
00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:09.600
between the Viking Age

237
00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:15.640
and the 19th century
peasant sailors of Häädemeeste.

238
00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:21.720
There is so much focus on
the ancient struggle for freedom

239
00:20:21.960 --> 00:20:27.000
and the Middle Ages as the era
in Estonian and Latvian history

240
00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:29.600
is left unseen
and that is the most important.

241
00:20:31.280 --> 00:20:33.880
And now it’s very different.

242
00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:37.840
Now we have the most
impressive ship of that era

243
00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:44.640
to show how the Hanseatic maritime
was a great deal for the land here.

244
00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:51.000
Cleaning a ship
is essentially like excavations.

245
00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:54.800
Because there is always
something coming out.

246
00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:57.560
By the way, here is a nail

247
00:20:57.640 --> 00:21:00.320
with a piece of wood.
Rather a nailhead.

248
00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:05.640
Those marked with an ‘x-ray’
will be x-rayed.

249
00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:09.520
It is often asked whether
we are restoring the cog.

250
00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:13.040
The answer is no,
we only preserve.

251
00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:14.800
There are several reasons,

252
00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:18.320
one of which is that we don’t know
what it might have looked like.

253
00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:20.360
Not all pieces exist.

254
00:21:20.440 --> 00:21:22.360
It’s like groping in the dark

255
00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:26.200
and we don’t know what will happen.

256
00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:27.920
We’ll see.

257
00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:34.120
I’m just checking the humidity
and temperature.

258
00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:39.720
Since the exhibition
is also about the cog,

259
00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:44.360
at least the part that I do
is about the cog. Only the cog.

260
00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:47.280
Do you also dream
about the cog at night?

261
00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:53.320
I dream about…

262
00:21:55.520 --> 00:21:59.240
I once dreamed that the construction
of the frame went badly.

263
00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:15.080
Hanseatic trade was a source
of wealth for the cities here.

264
00:22:15.560 --> 00:22:17.520
Hansa had no capital.

265
00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:22.080
The Hanseatic League
was a very amorphous organisation.

266
00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:25.240
It could be said that Hansa
is a network of cities

267
00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:29.320
and merchants in a weak
medieval central government.

268
00:22:30.600 --> 00:22:34.600
Neither Riga nor Tallinn can be said
to have joined Hansa.

269
00:22:34.680 --> 00:22:37.360
They are a part of Hansa
from the beginning.

270
00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:40.520
Because those merchants
who came with the Crusaders

271
00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:42.840
were nomadic Hanseatic merchants.

272
00:22:42.920 --> 00:22:45.200
At some point, they will
remain stationary

273
00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:47.720
if the situation there stabilises,

274
00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:51.560
and then they will be part
of this Hanseatic trading system.

275
00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:58.240
Today there is a clear distinction

276
00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:01.240
between cargo and warships
and passenger ships.

277
00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:04.000
At that time the distinction
was not very clear.

278
00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:06.320
Just a general-purpose ship.

279
00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:10.840
The main thing is that bigger
cargo vessels were needed

280
00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:14.800
and the ships were made
as big as they could.

281
00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:22.720
The cog brings the goods over
the sea from another Hanseatic city

282
00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:27.360
and then the goods are moved along
the coast or rivers in smaller ships.

283
00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:32.160
In terms of its
navigational capabilities,

284
00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:36.760
the big square rig is such that it
generally travels in the tailwind

285
00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:40.640
and its drift in the crosswind
is so great

286
00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:43.240
that it no longer sails well.

287
00:23:48.080 --> 00:23:50.240
There is a problem in Tallinn

288
00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:53.480
that large ships could not get
into the port very often

289
00:23:53.560 --> 00:23:55.760
because the port was too low.

290
00:23:55.840 --> 00:23:59.920
As a result, larger vessels
anchored in front of the port

291
00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.800
and the goods were
transported by smaller boats.

292
00:24:15.920 --> 00:24:18.200
I think it is a fairly
plausible assumption

293
00:24:18.280 --> 00:24:23.080
that the ship was anchored,

294
00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:28.520
hit by a storm and there was
not much shade at sea.

295
00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:31.760
Apparently the ship
was either washed ashore

296
00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:33.760
or there was some kind of leak.

297
00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:36.960
To avoid drowning, the team
drove the ship to the beach.

298
00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:54.440
And when the sea carries
a shipwreck to the beach,

299
00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:57.600
firstly it is cleaned up

300
00:24:57.680 --> 00:25:01.280
and secondly the waves and ice
will break it immediately.

301
00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:03.840
But if the shipwreck
happens suddenly,

302
00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:07.280
that the storm throws
a big pile of sand there as well,

303
00:25:07.360 --> 00:25:11.280
then it will be preserved
much better.

304
00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:15.160
No human remains
were found on the ship.

305
00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:17.560
So we assume that the crew escaped,

306
00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:21.760
but given how many
personal items were left behind,

307
00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:26.520
it can be assumed that the crew
had a fairly quick departure.

308
00:25:37.520 --> 00:25:43.440
THIRD CAPSULE:
GREAT COASTAL GATE

309
00:26:04.840 --> 00:26:10.440
A ground-penetrating radar survey
had been conducted in Fat Margaret,

310
00:26:10.520 --> 00:26:13.760
which revealed
that the whole courtyard

311
00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:16.360
is full of some structures
from edge to edge.

312
00:26:16.440 --> 00:26:20.280
GPR shows their depth
and signal intensity,

313
00:26:20.360 --> 00:26:22.480
but not what is there.

314
00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:25.720
And it was clear that it
would not be possible

315
00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:29.080
to build this museum
without an archaeologist.

316
00:26:29.320 --> 00:26:31.120
The construction
of Fat Margaret

317
00:26:31.200 --> 00:26:33.760
is related to the modernisation
of the city wall,

318
00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:37.320
especially for the needs
of the artillery or firearms era.

319
00:26:37.400 --> 00:26:40.320
If before that
a high wall was enough,

320
00:26:40.400 --> 00:26:42.920
then if decent cannons come,

321
00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:45.440
the towers are becoming
lower and more massive.

322
00:26:45.520 --> 00:26:47.480
It’s 11.30.

323
00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:51.440
Fat Margaret and its
eastern defensive wall,

324
00:26:51.520 --> 00:26:55.200
were actually built
in the town’s old rose garden,

325
00:26:55.280 --> 00:27:00.120
which was one of the earlier
public green spaces in the city.

326
00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:03.080
There was a beautiful
linden tree in the middle of it.

327
00:27:03.160 --> 00:27:08.640
And supposedly it was a pleasant
place to spend free time.

328
00:27:19.800 --> 00:27:24.280
Can you see anything ahead
in the tunnel?

329
00:27:25.120 --> 00:27:28.920
It ends in about 8 metres.

330
00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:33.960
Like Fat Margaret maybe.

331
00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:38.400
We don’t know exactly
where the medieval port was,

332
00:27:38.480 --> 00:27:44.080
but it is still in the direction
where Sadama street starts today.

333
00:27:45.520 --> 00:27:49.640
Over time,
from the first defensive wall,

334
00:27:49.720 --> 00:27:54.440
Fat Margaret, or should I say,
the Great Coastal Gate complex

335
00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:58.360
began to move towards the sea.
More gates were being built

336
00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:04.280
as the threats increased politically
and as the weaponry developed.

337
00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:06.800
Can you see the end?
- Yes.

338
00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:11.840
It’s going straight
in that direction.

339
00:28:15.880 --> 00:28:19.600
I think there’s rubbish
at the wall of Fat Margaret.

340
00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:24.280
Okay. Maybe it was closed

341
00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:28.320
with the construction
of Fat Margaret.

342
00:28:28.400 --> 00:28:30.400
Maybe once it functioned.

343
00:28:30.480 --> 00:28:33.040
The specific motivation
for building Fat Margaret

344
00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:35.520
is the fear of the Danish invasion.

345
00:28:35.600 --> 00:28:39.880
The Danes have repeatedly
raised the demand

346
00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:43.760
that Northern Estonia was a part
of the Kingdom of Denmark.

347
00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:45.080
We want it back.

348
00:28:45.160 --> 00:28:49.120
This is one of the reasons
why the port needs to be secured.

349
00:28:49.720 --> 00:28:52.880
It was indeed a new tower
at the Great Coastal Gate.

350
00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:58.360
And then it was named Rosenkrantz
in the 17th century.

351
00:28:58.440 --> 00:29:03.800
It wasn’t until the admiralty began
storing its rations

352
00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:07.680
in the Fat Margaret complex around
the beginning of the 19th century

353
00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:10.080
that it probably became known
as Fat Margaret

354
00:29:10.160 --> 00:29:12.400
in connection with that food.

355
00:29:13.880 --> 00:29:15.840
There is a bottom here as well.

356
00:29:15.920 --> 00:29:18.720
There is about half a shovelful here.

357
00:29:20.040 --> 00:29:22.800
What we discovered then was that

358
00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:26.360
with the defensive wall
of the front gate

359
00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:31.760
a rainwater canal had been built,
lined and covered with limestone,

360
00:29:31.840 --> 00:29:35.480
and probably this canal led

361
00:29:35.560 --> 00:29:41.560
the excess water from the moat
of the Tower Square to the sea.

362
00:29:43.680 --> 00:29:47.760
All this rainwater drainage
system that came out,

363
00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:51.280
a passage that goes through
under the defensive wall,

364
00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:56.720
cobblestones from two different eras,
which can be seen in the courtyard.

365
00:29:57.080 --> 00:29:59.080
The wall of Margareta’s front gate,

366
00:29:59.160 --> 00:30:02.560
which can be seen
on the ground floor of the tower.

367
00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:04.920
Actually, very, very much.

368
00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:06.360
And it was expected.

369
00:30:06.440 --> 00:30:08.880
It would have been
naive to start construction

370
00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:12.320
and say that we
will not find anything.

371
00:30:12.400 --> 00:30:14.480
Life is not that simple.

372
00:30:21.360 --> 00:30:27.400
FOURTH CAPSULE:
SHIP MODEL

373
00:31:06.840 --> 00:31:09.000
Come out already…

374
00:31:09.920 --> 00:31:11.920
Got it.

375
00:31:13.600 --> 00:31:16.600
The model of the oldest ship we have

376
00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:19.200
is from the beginning
of the 19th century.

377
00:31:19.280 --> 00:31:22.040
One is a model of
a short-distance cargo ship

378
00:31:22.120 --> 00:31:26.160
and the other is a model of
a long-distance cargo ship.

379
00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:29.560
We bought them from the
Estonian model master, Jüri Žurba,

380
00:31:29.640 --> 00:31:33.760
who is one of the best builders
of sailing ship models in Estonia.

381
00:31:36.160 --> 00:31:39.360
In fact, I am
a precision mechanic by education

382
00:31:39.440 --> 00:31:42.800
and worked with
precise things all the time.

383
00:31:42.880 --> 00:31:47.720
And such dedication
was already in the blood.

384
00:31:47.800 --> 00:31:53.440
Around 1970 after
graduating from university,

385
00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:56.960
I came home and then randomly
bought a German book,

386
00:31:57.040 --> 00:31:59.360
encyclopedia of ship modelling.

387
00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:02.240
And I started trying.

388
00:32:02.440 --> 00:32:05.480
So it went

389
00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:12.040
and today it has been
over 50 years and about 180 models.

390
00:32:17.040 --> 00:32:20.400
I really like woodworking.

391
00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:27.280
Wood is such a warm, living material.
It has a soul, so to speak.

392
00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:31.880
It’s like an artist’s work.
He needs to know the colours.

393
00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:35.360
If you make the right ship model,

394
00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:40.360
you need to know this wood,
its property, warmth and soul.

395
00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:45.440
It also has carpentry work,
artist work, jewellery work

396
00:32:45.520 --> 00:32:49.160
and a very wide historical study.

397
00:32:49.240 --> 00:32:51.840
That was a bit more fun.

398
00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:56.320
40% of this model’s
price and workload

399
00:32:56.400 --> 00:33:00.840
takes the research and drawings.
Surprising, but it really is so.

400
00:33:00.920 --> 00:33:05.280
With the Hioma model I started
building and that I’m finishing now.

401
00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:09.880
The only thing I had was
a picture of the model, a painting.

402
00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:16.480
Overall dimensions, widths, lengths.
This vessel had to be reconstructed.

403
00:33:17.720 --> 00:33:20.760
That was the standard drawing.

404
00:33:22.840 --> 00:33:29.320
This is an album from Russia
of typical ships, their projects.

405
00:33:29.400 --> 00:33:31.680
That’s what I started with.

406
00:33:33.960 --> 00:33:38.880
Here’s the sailing equipment,
but here came some changes

407
00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:42.480
Hioma is the first ship
built in Estonia

408
00:33:42.560 --> 00:33:45.960
to sail around Cape Horn in 1854.

409
00:33:46.040 --> 00:33:48.360
It was one of the first in Estonia

410
00:33:48.440 --> 00:33:51.320
and will be featured
separately in the exhibition.

411
00:33:52.920 --> 00:33:56.960
I calculated the body
and all the dimensions.

412
00:33:57.040 --> 00:34:02.240
And then I had to look up
all the parts of the deck.

413
00:34:02.320 --> 00:34:08.560
At that time, what mechanisms,
pumps, winches were in use.

414
00:34:14.080 --> 00:34:16.680
When I got the archive,

415
00:34:16.760 --> 00:34:20.480
then I combined and placed
the deck nicely on the ship

416
00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:22.480
and somehow I managed to do it.

417
00:34:35.080 --> 00:34:39.480
This is done.
Let’s fix this side as well.

418
00:34:47.720 --> 00:34:50.520
One, two, three… This one.

419
00:34:50.600 --> 00:34:53.920
We had the biggest gap
in steam shipping,

420
00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:58.080
because in 1940,
when Estonia was occupied,

421
00:34:58.160 --> 00:35:01.120
just before that
there were 177 steamers

422
00:35:01.200 --> 00:35:04.440
in the Estonian cargo ship register.

423
00:35:04.520 --> 00:35:07.640
But we had just under 20 steamers

424
00:35:07.720 --> 00:35:10.560
and most of them were
special-purpose vessels,

425
00:35:10.640 --> 00:35:12.240
either tugs or icebreakers.

426
00:35:12.320 --> 00:35:16.640
Now it is actually comparable
in size to the Estonian beach.

427
00:35:18.720 --> 00:35:22.360
Both models are made by Håkan.

428
00:35:23.680 --> 00:35:26.760
There are not many
model masters in Estonia

429
00:35:26.840 --> 00:35:29.040
who can craft in museum quality,

430
00:35:29.120 --> 00:35:31.960
which meant international
public procurement,

431
00:35:32.040 --> 00:35:35.800
and in addition
to Estonian model masters,

432
00:35:35.880 --> 00:35:39.680
model masters from
Åland Islands and England

433
00:35:39.760 --> 00:35:42.520
also made different
ship models for us.

434
00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:50.160
When I grew up

435
00:35:50.240 --> 00:35:54.640
in Mariehamn in the 1950s,

436
00:35:54.720 --> 00:36:00.560
we lived quite close to the harbour
and were often there with friends.

437
00:36:00.640 --> 00:36:05.560
In addition, the fathers
of many of my friends

438
00:36:05.640 --> 00:36:09.760
were captains or machinists.

439
00:36:09.840 --> 00:36:16.560
And we went aboard those ships.
And I have a shipping background.

440
00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:22.160
I have worked in shipping companies
and shipyards all my life.

441
00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:33.600
FIFTH CAPSULE:
GREETINGS FROM SOVIET ESTONIA

442
00:37:09.040 --> 00:37:15.920
It was in 1979 when we had
Polish construction workers

443
00:37:16.000 --> 00:37:21.480
and they put this capsule
in one of these pillars

444
00:37:21.560 --> 00:37:25.440
when they renovated the tower
and built these partition ceilings,

445
00:37:25.520 --> 00:37:28.120
because it was
completely empty before.

446
00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:32.800
And that’s why we see here
I think the Polish language

447
00:37:32.880 --> 00:37:36.360
and the names
of this construction team,

448
00:37:36.440 --> 00:37:39.680
but the Maritime Museum
itself was much older

449
00:37:39.760 --> 00:37:42.280
and here is a list
of those who worked

450
00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:44.800
at the Maritime Museum at that time.

451
00:37:48.040 --> 00:37:51.720
It was damaged
and must have been drilled through.

452
00:37:51.800 --> 00:37:55.360
Yes, when it was found
from the inside of the post.

453
00:37:55.440 --> 00:37:59.320
After all, no one puts
a cornerstone capsule

454
00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:02.600
with such a definite hope that it
will be found at some point.

455
00:38:02.680 --> 00:38:07.120
Better if it’s not found.
But we found it after all.

456
00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:39.360
The festive part is that you put
the old capsule in with the new one.

457
00:38:39.440 --> 00:38:43.520
Today, it is a rare coincidence

458
00:38:43.600 --> 00:38:47.680
that we put back the time capsule

459
00:38:47.760 --> 00:38:53.320
that is usually placed
according to the good old tradition.

460
00:38:53.400 --> 00:38:58.760
We put it back, but since it’s not
quite waterproof anymore,

461
00:38:58.840 --> 00:39:02.840
we put this capsule in a new capsule

462
00:39:02.920 --> 00:39:08.000
and then put it back in almost
the same place where it was found

463
00:39:08.080 --> 00:39:11.400
and where it should be
from the beginning.

464
00:39:11.480 --> 00:39:13.400
Let’s put it in now.

465
00:39:26.160 --> 00:39:27.720
Here it is.

466
00:39:27.800 --> 00:39:30.000
If all goes well,

467
00:39:30.080 --> 00:39:34.640
then there are plans to invite
to the new opening of the museum

468
00:39:34.720 --> 00:39:41.320
the architect of the construction
project of Fat Margaret from the 1970s.

469
00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:48.360
Jerzy Tusiacki. Let’s see how
it goes. Hopefully, he won’t…

470
00:39:48.440 --> 00:39:50.760
…he will have a creative attitude,

471
00:39:50.840 --> 00:39:53.480
because a lot has changed

472
00:39:53.560 --> 00:39:57.080
since he made his drawings

473
00:39:57.160 --> 00:39:59.720
for the reconstruction
of Fat Margaret.

474
00:40:01.880 --> 00:40:07.360
SIXTH CAPSULE:
THE NEW MUSEUM

475
00:41:51.080 --> 00:41:54.560
I’ll check.
Maybe I have it open here.

476
00:41:59.920 --> 00:42:03.240
This was the model that we

477
00:42:04.080 --> 00:42:09.320
photographed with great effort
while demolition took place.

478
00:42:09.400 --> 00:42:14.080
It consists of such fragments,

479
00:42:14.160 --> 00:42:20.280
as there used to be
a showcase or a wall.

480
00:42:20.800 --> 00:42:22.920
We had to combine.

481
00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:29.840
What remained of this exhibition
was a donut-shaped space

482
00:42:29.920 --> 00:42:32.840
that had to accommodate a lot.

483
00:42:33.080 --> 00:42:36.280
For us, these ship models
were certainly

484
00:42:36.360 --> 00:42:40.080
one of the main items around
which to build the exhibition.

485
00:42:40.160 --> 00:42:44.840
The overarching theme was
a great deal of white and metal,

486
00:42:44.920 --> 00:42:49.320
that it is somehow so
symbolic of white ships.

487
00:42:49.400 --> 00:42:53.080
We did not try to catch up
with the current trend,

488
00:42:53.160 --> 00:42:55.800
but rather to remain neutral.

489
00:42:56.360 --> 00:42:59.280
The heritage protection
position was that

490
00:42:59.360 --> 00:43:03.200
everything you see in the old
must be seen in the future.

491
00:43:03.520 --> 00:43:09.040
I think we brought
a lot of light to the exhibition.

492
00:43:09.120 --> 00:43:11.840
And another theme is a kind of cleansing

493
00:43:11.920 --> 00:43:15.080
that if before
it was rather fragmentary,

494
00:43:15.160 --> 00:43:21.880
then at the moment we are trying
to keep and add readability,

495
00:43:21.960 --> 00:43:27.200
that you come in and see
that here are the ship models.

496
00:43:27.280 --> 00:43:30.800
At the highest level,
you don’t have to look any further.

497
00:43:30.880 --> 00:43:36.800
And then you can constantly
delve into smaller topic blocks.

498
00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:40.520
There must be a great contrast
between the new and the old,

499
00:43:40.600 --> 00:43:47.520
and then this a bit dark
and tattered limestone wall,

500
00:43:47.600 --> 00:43:53.080
a little gloomy and mystical,
that then the new and bright

501
00:43:53.160 --> 00:43:57.160
and white and clear…

502
00:43:57.240 --> 00:44:00.560
There wasn’t very much
an example needed.

503
00:44:00.640 --> 00:44:06.240
One did not have to search
very far in mind.

504
00:44:06.840 --> 00:44:08.600
FAT MARGARET

505
00:44:14.320 --> 00:44:16.880
<i>The joyful event</i>
is acknowledged by those

506
00:44:16.960 --> 00:44:20.000
<i>who have demanded bread</i>
from the sea for decades.

507
00:44:20.080 --> 00:44:22.160
<i>Sailor and writer Sergo</i>

508
00:44:22.240 --> 00:44:26.520
<i>and Captain Leemet -</i>
writer and reminder.

509
00:44:27.720 --> 00:44:31.040
<i>Uno Laul - author of the</i>
Estonian maritime dictionary.

510
00:44:31.120 --> 00:44:33.760
<i>Johannes Konga, Teodor Lõõbas.</i>

511
00:44:33.840 --> 00:44:37.440
<i>Every man is a piece of the living</i>
history of Estonian shipping.

512
00:44:38.080 --> 00:44:41.920
This museum, which opened in 1981
and the exhibition,

513
00:44:42.000 --> 00:44:47.440
which lasted until
the last major remodelling,

514
00:44:47.520 --> 00:44:51.280
was a classic in a very good way.

515
00:44:51.360 --> 00:44:54.680
We played a lot on
ship models even then,

516
00:44:54.760 --> 00:44:57.880
and we will continue
with the theme of ship models

517
00:44:57.960 --> 00:45:00.360
in this new exhibition as well.

518
00:45:00.440 --> 00:45:03.400
<i>The museum has many young visitors.</i>

519
00:45:03.480 --> 00:45:07.600
<i>Probably what they see here</i>
will open up the gates of the ocean.

520
00:45:07.960 --> 00:45:10.400
When we talk about
people with disabilities,

521
00:45:10.480 --> 00:45:13.800
then it was completely
remote that time.

522
00:45:13.880 --> 00:45:17.040
We have made this one of

523
00:45:17.120 --> 00:45:20.840
our priorities when creating
the current exhibition.

524
00:45:24.080 --> 00:45:26.880
In my case,
when someone hands me

525
00:45:26.960 --> 00:45:30.720
for example, a soft toy animal
and asks what animal it is,

526
00:45:30.800 --> 00:45:35.280
I generally do not know.
Because in 99% of cases,

527
00:45:35.360 --> 00:45:39.360
such as when talking about
a cat or a dog or a giraffe,

528
00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:42.400
you only hear the word
and do not see it on the picture.

529
00:45:42.480 --> 00:45:45.640
You may know he has a long neck,
but that is it.

530
00:45:45.720 --> 00:45:50.480
So it is with ships and all the other
similar things that you can touch.

531
00:45:50.760 --> 00:45:54.760
Someone can describe to me that
the ship is broken, it is a wreck.

532
00:45:54.840 --> 00:45:57.880
But of course,
if you touch this 3D model,

533
00:45:57.960 --> 00:46:02.600
then you can tell
that it is rough and…

534
00:46:02.680 --> 00:46:05.560
Well, here you can see those
so-called ribs or boards

535
00:46:05.640 --> 00:46:07.880
sticking out of here

536
00:46:07.960 --> 00:46:10.640
and here it is understood
that it is obviously not

537
00:46:10.720 --> 00:46:15.920
the whole ship and the upper part
is completely missing.

538
00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:20.960
The Estonian Association
of the Blind was asked

539
00:46:21.040 --> 00:46:26.560
how to make the museum accessible
to the visually impaired.

540
00:46:26.640 --> 00:46:29.720
And how to install
a guide road on the floor,

541
00:46:29.800 --> 00:46:32.960
i.e. a relief track that
a person who cannot see

542
00:46:33.040 --> 00:46:35.640
can move with a white stick.

543
00:46:35.720 --> 00:46:39.000
And then the other side
was the content side,

544
00:46:39.080 --> 00:46:44.960
where we focused both on
describing the different exhibits,

545
00:46:45.040 --> 00:46:48.880
and then on the opportunity
to touch them by hand.

546
00:46:48.960 --> 00:46:54.440
For example, this cog here
is printed out in 3D,

547
00:46:54.520 --> 00:46:57.920
the top surface of the deck
has like polka dots,

548
00:46:58.000 --> 00:47:03.040
while the sides have long stripes
like wooden boards.

549
00:47:03.120 --> 00:47:07.960
Another interesting thing we were
working on was the sail here.

550
00:47:08.040 --> 00:47:11.680
The sailcloth must be strong.

551
00:47:11.760 --> 00:47:15.960
We tried to get it
in such a position,

552
00:47:16.040 --> 00:47:19.600
to give the impression of a sail,
and not just a piece of clothing.

553
00:47:19.680 --> 00:47:24.000
Like the wind is blowing in the sail.

554
00:47:41.320 --> 00:47:45.920
We are telling the story
of Estonian maritime affairs

555
00:47:46.000 --> 00:47:47.640
through different types of ships

556
00:47:47.720 --> 00:47:50.000
and the development
of maritime technology,

557
00:47:50.080 --> 00:47:55.400
and so the floors are
divided from the cog hall,

558
00:47:55.480 --> 00:47:58.680
where in between are sailing ships,
steamships and motorboats

559
00:47:58.760 --> 00:48:01.000
and it’s that simple.

560
00:48:02.280 --> 00:48:06.480
The most important change
is the structure of this exhibition.

561
00:48:06.560 --> 00:48:11.080
If our former exposition was
very clearly chronological,

562
00:48:11.160 --> 00:48:13.640
followed historical events,

563
00:48:13.720 --> 00:48:16.320
then today we could say
that our exposition

564
00:48:16.400 --> 00:48:19.680
is semi-chronological
and semi-thematic.

565
00:48:19.760 --> 00:48:22.760
When we talk about steam shipping,

566
00:48:22.840 --> 00:48:25.240
it is one of the
biggest breakthroughs

567
00:48:25.320 --> 00:48:27.840
in shipping and shipping history.

568
00:48:27.920 --> 00:48:34.200
In the past, sailing was very much
affected by the forces of nature

569
00:48:34.280 --> 00:48:37.040
and it changed the crews of ships.

570
00:48:37.120 --> 00:48:39.760
There had to be mechanics, stokers

571
00:48:39.840 --> 00:48:42.680
and very different
professions on board.

572
00:48:43.600 --> 00:48:46.440
We have also discussed
ourselves that, in fact,

573
00:48:46.520 --> 00:48:49.040
the transition from
steamers to motor vessels

574
00:48:49.120 --> 00:48:52.600
was a much smaller technical change,

575
00:48:52.680 --> 00:48:56.400
which is why we wanted to highlight

576
00:48:56.480 --> 00:48:59.440
the nuance of the
industrial revolution.

577
00:49:05.840 --> 00:49:10.400
On this floor, the models
are divided into three.

578
00:49:10.480 --> 00:49:15.400
There are merchant ships,
fishing boats and passenger ships.

579
00:49:20.360 --> 00:49:21.600
Testing everything.

580
00:49:21.680 --> 00:49:24.720
<i>Dear visitors,</i>
we ask for your attention.

581
00:49:24.800 --> 00:49:26.520
<i>Due to the state of emergency…</i>

582
00:49:26.600 --> 00:49:29.200
When discussing
with the chief curator,

583
00:49:29.280 --> 00:49:36.280
we had to decide what to
highlight from the most modern ones.

584
00:49:36.360 --> 00:49:41.640
Because we have wanted to show
the changes that have taken place.

585
00:49:45.680 --> 00:49:50.160
The problem of dealing with
the most recent history is

586
00:49:50.240 --> 00:49:52.200
that everything is changing.

587
00:49:52.280 --> 00:49:55.160
You never know

588
00:49:55.240 --> 00:50:00.120
what the most important event of
2019 is that should be highlighted.

589
00:50:00.200 --> 00:50:05.200
I hope that it could be that

590
00:50:05.280 --> 00:50:08.680
the <i>Riigikogu</i> passed
this amendment to the law

591
00:50:08.760 --> 00:50:14.240
in order to bring ships
back to the Estonian flag.

592
00:50:14.320 --> 00:50:19.280
It could be the significant event
in maritime affairs this year,

593
00:50:19.360 --> 00:50:22.560
but you’ll never know.

594
00:50:22.640 --> 00:50:26.280
Dealing with recent history
is hard work.

595
00:52:38.760 --> 00:52:42.360
That part wasn’t here before,
it’s been dug in here.

596
00:52:42.440 --> 00:52:47.120
This is one of the foundation walls
of the front gate that came out.

597
00:52:47.200 --> 00:52:51.680
Again probably
a beautiful 15th century wall.

598
00:52:52.720 --> 00:52:55.400
I especially like this
choice of shoes.

599
00:52:55.480 --> 00:52:57.000
Pretty well preserved.

600
00:52:57.080 --> 00:53:01.320
Yes, it was quite difficult
to collect these.

601
00:53:01.400 --> 00:53:06.720
Go straight ahead, your group leader
will take you on a journey. - Okay.

602
00:53:06.800 --> 00:53:08.560
We have two opening ceremonies

603
00:53:08.640 --> 00:53:11.880
and there are really more than
a thousand people in a day.

604
00:53:14.640 --> 00:53:17.480
Quite a big party…

605
00:53:25.560 --> 00:53:29.200
But we have dedicated a lot to it,
and it is quite simple.

606
00:53:29.280 --> 00:53:31.800
Downstairs there is a medieval cog,

607
00:53:31.880 --> 00:53:35.400
on the second floor are sailing
ships, on the third are steamers.

608
00:53:35.800 --> 00:53:37.640
We call it the cog,

609
00:53:37.720 --> 00:53:42.200
but we do not know how
it was called in medieval times.

610
00:53:42.520 --> 00:53:46.960
Some called it just a big ship,

611
00:53:47.040 --> 00:53:50.720
some called it the cog
or something else.

612
00:53:50.800 --> 00:53:53.480
Even now, if you look
at these wooden planks

613
00:53:53.560 --> 00:53:56.000
and then think that
they have been cut down

614
00:53:56.080 --> 00:53:59.600
one generation before we had
the St. George’s Night Uprising.

615
00:53:59.680 --> 00:54:05.600
Then you realise the value
and uniqueness of all this.

616
00:54:08.720 --> 00:54:13.680
There are pictures
and stories of shipmasters

617
00:54:13.760 --> 00:54:16.520
in this inner circle.

618
00:54:27.200 --> 00:54:30.360
At the centre of the exposition
of the sailing ship floor

619
00:54:30.440 --> 00:54:32.040
is the mast of ship Triinu,

620
00:54:32.120 --> 00:54:37.240
which was an old
Estonian sailing ship.

621
00:54:37.320 --> 00:54:39.840
Nice that finally there is a place

622
00:54:39.920 --> 00:54:43.440
where you can leave the child playing
and see the exhibit.

623
00:54:43.520 --> 00:54:45.760
It’s always good.

624
00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:48.640
People were used to getting
to the port at random times.

625
00:54:48.720 --> 00:54:51.160
But the steamer gave the opportunity

626
00:54:51.240 --> 00:54:54.240
to leave, for example, at 2 o’clock
and so it was.

627
00:54:54.320 --> 00:54:56.800
And who was late was left behind.

628
00:54:56.880 --> 00:55:00.920
Is this a permanent exhibition?
- Yes, this is permanent.

629
00:55:28.720 --> 00:55:30.000
Dear guests,

630
00:55:30.080 --> 00:55:33.560
Head of the Maritime Museum,
Mr Urmas Dresen.

631
00:55:41.920 --> 00:55:44.360
You can assume that I am very happy.

632
00:55:44.520 --> 00:55:48.400
Not only me. The whole
Maritime Museum is very happy,

633
00:55:48.520 --> 00:55:53.280
that we can stand here
under the stem of the cog,

634
00:55:53.360 --> 00:55:57.880
walk around the cog,
walk in the tower

635
00:55:57.960 --> 00:56:02.240
and feel that something
great has been done.

636
00:56:53.840 --> 00:56:57.160
Translated by Kelli Kuldvee
